== The 120-Question Master Bank — Complete Solutions

#sol-item(1, "A")[
  *Chapter 1.* Plant cells have cellulose walls and are autotrophic; animal cells have no wall and obtain food by ingestion.
]

#sol-item(2, "C")[
  *Chapter 2.* A granum is a stack of thylakoid discs; cristae are the folds of the inner mitochondrial membrane.
]

#sol-item(3, "B")[
  *Chapter 3.* Spindle microtubules attached to the centromeres shorten and draw the chromatids pole-ward.
]

#sol-item(4, "D")[
  *Chapter 4.* Air in a closed greenhouse can fall below the natural 0.03 % CO₂ level; topping it up keeps photosynthesis running while light is strong.
]

#sol-item(5, "D")[
  *Chapter 5.* Microvilli add a further fold to the villus surface; the two together multiply the area many times.
]

#sol-item(6, "A")[
  *Chapter 6.* Sugar is transported in the phloem; the xylem stream moves water and dissolved minerals upwards.
]

#sol-item(7, "B")[
  *Chapter 7.* Higher pressure is needed to push blood through the systemic circulation than through the short pulmonary route.
]

#sol-item(8, "C")[
  *Chapter 8.* This is the first line of defence of the respiratory tract; smoking paralyses the cilia.
]

#sol-item(9, "B")[
  *Chapter 9.* The membrane has pores small enough to hold back large molecules and cells while letting the filtrate through.
]

#sol-item(10, "C")[
  *Chapter 10.* The axial skeleton forms the central axis; the appendicular skeleton is the limbs and their girdles.
]

#sol-item(11, "A")[
  *Chapter 1.* Movement, Respiration, *Sensitivity* (irritability), Growth, Reproduction, Excretion, Nutrition.
]

#sol-item(12, "D")[
  *Chapter 2.* The nuclear membrane is perforated by pores through which RNA and ribosomal subunits travel outwards and proteins travel inwards.
]

#sol-item(13, "C")[
  *Chapter 3.* Chromatids are copied exactly, so both daughters carry the same chromosome complement as the parent.
]

#sol-item(14, "A")[
  *Chapter 4.* Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue-violet light and reflects green — which is why leaves look green.
]

#sol-item(15, "B")[
  *Chapter 5.* Chewing, churning and emulsification break food into smaller pieces but do not change its chemistry.
]

#sol-item(16, "D")[
  *Chapter 6.* Cohesion-tension theory: evaporation at the top creates a tension that is transmitted down an unbroken column of water held together by cohesion.
]

#sol-item(17, "D")[
